WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:06.190
In 1984, French philosopher Jacques Derrida
defined nuclear war as a "fabulously textual"

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:10.840
reality about which "one can only talk and
write about" because "it has not yet, and,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.650
in a sense cannot ever take place, since if
it did, there would be no place remaining."

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.110
Clear and concise.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:22.010
In the common imagination, but also in practice,
there is only one way to attempt to escape

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:26.970
the disastrous effects of a nuclear detonation:
holing up in a fallout shelter.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.839
A bunker is nothing more than an underground
shelter that, in a wartime context, is exploited

00:00:31.839 --> 00:00:34.950
to protect armaments, resources, and military
personnel.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:40.840
This was true, at least, until August 1945,
when the world verified the existence of a

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.000
weapon capable of exterminating the human
race: the atomic bomb.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.670
According to American professor David L. Pike,
it was precisely the Cold War, with which

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:55.059
the danger of a global nuclear holocaust was
associated, that gave rise to the so-called

00:00:55.059 --> 00:01:00.559
bunker fantasy, that is, the widespread belief
that bomb shelters are necessary for the safety

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:01.559
of mankind.

00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:02.559
Bunker fantasy exorcises death, and generates
hope even towards a future of death and destruction.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:03.559
After all, this is precisely the function
of the post-apocalyptic genre, well represented

00:01:03.559 --> 00:01:04.559
by video games such as Fallout.

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:05.559
Well, someone has taken this concept literally.

00:01:05.559 --> 00:01:08.340
We are talking about Switzerland, the most
bunkered country in the world.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:15.240
With its about 370.000 private fallout shelters,
plus 9.000 public ones intended for those

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.000
who don’t have the budget to install them
in their own homes, on paper Switzerland is

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.280
able to protect as many as 9 million people
from radiation, or more than 100% of its entire

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:27.280
population.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.280
Impressive, huh?

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.280
But how did it happen?

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.090
It all starts with a pre-Cold War bunker fantasy.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:34.530
We are at the beginning of the Second World
War.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:39.060
France has been defeated by the Third Reich,
which towers in the north and has annexed

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.060
Austria.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.329
To the south, on the other hand, is fascist
Italy.

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:47.280
Although Switzerland declared neutrality,
rising border tensions prompted the authorities

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:48.950
to prepare for a fight.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:56.180
On July 25, 1940, General Henri Guisan suggested
making the Alps, Switzerland's natural barrier,

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.259
the largest fortress in Europe.

00:01:58.259 --> 00:02:03.340
The plan is as follows: abandon the northern
and southern borders, as well as the center

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.869
of the country, to allow the army to hole
up in a network of military shelters built

00:02:07.869 --> 00:02:08.869
into the rocks.

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:14.200
In fact, the so-called National Redoubt strategy,
also known as Schweizer Reduit or Réduit

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.290
national, had been nagging at the minds of
the Swiss government since the 1880s, and

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:23.379
World War II only served as a fuse for the
project to be carried out.

00:02:23.379 --> 00:02:28.370
This Alpine fortification consisted of three
main bastions, the Gotthard, Saint-Maurice

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:35.010
and Sargans complexes, overseeing 70 other
mountain citadels and 10,000 bunkers capable

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:40.700
of housing hundreds of men each, protected
by armored doors and stocked with food, weapons

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.090
and ammunition.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:47.629
According to an estimate reported by the New
York Times in 1999, the Redoubt cost Switzerland

00:02:47.629 --> 00:02:49.200
$10 billion.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.370
Judged in retrospect, this strategy would
be considered, yes, a huge deterrent designed

00:02:54.370 --> 00:03:00.230
to discourage Axis forces from invading the
Alps, but also an absurd idea.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:04.129
In fact, "reducing" the country, in the view
of the Swiss generals, meant sacrificing the

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:07.670
central plains where the vast majority of
the population lived.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:12.209
In short, it was a calculation based on the
survival of the armed forces alone.

00:03:12.209 --> 00:03:16.920
Yet the obsession of the fortress between
the Alps would survive in a different form

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.620
after the Second World War.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:23.510
In the presence of a far more deadly threat
than air raids and tank advances, namely the

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:29.370
atomic bomb, the Swiss public became increasingly
aware of the need to protect itself from all-out

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:30.370
war.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:34.650
It wasn’t only the Iron Curtain that haunted
Swiss military strategists, but the very real

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:37.470
risk that the Cold War could soon...heat up.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:43.890
As early as the Korean War of 1950-1953, the
Swiss Federal Council, or the seven-member

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:49.360
directorate that performs the functions of
both government and head of state, ruled that

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:53.700
every newly constructed building should be
equipped with an air raid shelter.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:04:00.010
Then, in 1956, the Suez crisis prompted the
Federal Council to raise civil defense to

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:05.400
the status of a constitutional right and the
ultimate responsibility of state authorities.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.780
That said, defense finds constitutional protection
in Article 2, in the part that states that

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:14.430
"the Confederation [...] safeguards [...] the
security of the country."

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:18.600
But two additional historical contingencies
would prompt the Swiss government to apply

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:24.060
drastic measures in preparation for a global
conflict that seemed inevitable at the time:

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:30.370
the building of the Berlin Wall in 1961 and
the Cuban Missile Crisis in October 1962.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:36.039
Not surprisingly, also in 1962, the Swiss
parliament created a Federal Office for Civil

00:04:36.039 --> 00:04:42.070
Protection, abbreviated to FOCP, still in
existence today, whose tasks are to inform

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:47.389
and alert citizens in case of danger, and
coordinate possible rescue operations.

00:04:47.389 --> 00:04:53.639
In October 1963, by unanimous vote of both
houses of parliament, the Federal Act on Civil

00:04:53.639 --> 00:04:55.910
Defense Construction was passed.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:05:00.510
The latter provided for the installation of
public fallout bunkers in every settlements

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.229
with more than 1.000 inhabitants.

00:05:02.229 --> 00:05:04.160
And not only that.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:10.380
Under Article 46, all property owners were
to construct and equip their own bomb shelter.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:14.920
This was a rather ambitious provision, but
it was dictated by well-established ideological

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:16.229
reasons.

00:05:16.229 --> 00:05:22.230
According to historian Silvia Berger Ziauddin,
Geistige Landesverteidigung, that is the "national

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:27.850
intellectual defense," implied that bunkers
had to protect all fundamental Swiss values:

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:30.440
federalism, freedom, independence and neutrality.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:34.669
At the same time, however, it was the latter
that was sacrificed.

00:05:34.669 --> 00:05:40.160
Although Switzerland never became a NATO member,
by the dawn of the 1960s the Swiss Federal

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:44.460
Council clearly recognized who the enemy was,
and that was communism.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:50.370
A year before the parliament in Bern enacted
the Federal law, the FOCP was given a council

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:55.610
of ten experts to figure out how to build
bunkers that would protect people not only

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:58.840
from a nuclear blast but also from the resulting
radiation.

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.800
Yes, no one actually knew how to make one.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:06.590
At the time, the only theoretical compendium
available in the West about the atomic bomb

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:12.190
was the book The Effects of Nuclear Weapons
written by physicist Samuel Glasstone, published

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:17.690
by the U.S. government in 1957, and based
on experiments conducted by the Pentagon in

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:19.510
Nevada and the Bikini Atoll.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:27.539
In July 1963, the Swiss FOCP's board of experts
invited a number of U.S. and West German researchers

00:06:27.539 --> 00:06:33.000
to the Swiss Federal Institute of Technology
in Zurich to design in cooperation with Washington

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.670
a working prototype of a fallout bunker.

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:38.470
There and then, the conference turned out
to be a failure.

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:43.060
Yet in October, the Swiss parliament would
still pass the Federal Act on Civil Defense

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:44.060
Construction.

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:45.060
This wasn’t a reckless move.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:50.220
In fact, during the Zurich conference, the
director of the Swiss Federal Institute of

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:55.620
Technology, military engineer Ernst Basler,
came into contact with the Naval Radiological

00:06:55.620 --> 00:07:01.009
Defense Laboratory in San Francisco, Stanford
University and MIT in Cambridge.

00:07:01.009 --> 00:07:07.410
Thanks to these connections, between 1964
and 1970, dozens of Swiss scientists traveled

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:12.539
to the United States, gained access to their
technologies and, once back home, contributed

00:07:12.539 --> 00:07:16.430
their knowledge to what we might call a veritable
shelter boom.

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:21.300
Within just six years, the number of private
fallout bunkers in Switzerland reached one

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:26.160
hundred thousand, capable of protecting about
50 percent of the population.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:31.030
An astonishing achievement, considering that
the United States itself has never been able

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:35.379
to implement a structural program to spread
the underground with bunkers.

00:07:35.379 --> 00:07:41.130
It may seem like nonsense to you, but the
success and speed of the Swiss shelters lies...in

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:42.910
not having conducted experiments.

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:48.789
Basler and the FOCP's board of experts had
to act quickly to fulfill the provisions of

00:07:48.789 --> 00:07:52.199
the 1963 Construction Act, while trying to
keep costs down.

00:07:52.199 --> 00:07:56.250
With a world war considered imminent, there
was no time for testing.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:08:01.530
So, scientists put together all the data they
had collected on the effects of a nuclear

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:04.979
explosion to design cost-benefit optimal countermeasures.

00:08:04.979 --> 00:08:10.620
Plants, walls, doors, floors, and ventilation
systems were designed according to the following

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:16.069
calculation: with an estimated investment
of 1.000 Swiss francs per person, Switzerland

00:08:16.069 --> 00:08:21.659
could have bunkers capable of saving from
an atomic detonation 90 percent of all those

00:08:21.659 --> 00:08:23.009
who took shelter in them.

00:08:23.009 --> 00:08:28.879
The culmination of bunkering occurred starting
in 1971, when the Federal Act on Civil Protection

00:08:28.879 --> 00:08:31.340
was extended to all settlements.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:35.140
Each private shelter would be 70 percent publicly
funded.

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:39.419
If one were to point to the greatest work
of the entire fortification process in the

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:43.640
Swiss Confederation, one couldn’t fail to
mention The Sonnenberg Tunnel.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:49.470
Opened in 1976 in Lucerne, hidden underneath
a playground, one of the world's largest civilian

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:54.570
bunkers cost about 40 million francs, was
designed to extend along three kilometers

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:59.540
of tunnels located on the highway linking
Basel to Lugano, and to accommodate about

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:00.850
20 thousand people.

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:06.550
The Bunker is a seven-story building stretching
downwards, and in the minds of the FOCP would

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:12.860
have required about 700 employees to run its
beds, command station, hospital, kitchen,

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:14.870
laundry and prison.

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:17.060
In short, a real Vault built by Vault tec.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:21.529
It's just too bad that, right now, it’s
not as massive as planned.

00:09:21.529 --> 00:09:26.399
In 1987, a year after the Chernobyl accident,
the authorities conducted a general test to

00:09:26.399 --> 00:09:30.920
evaluate the operation of the Cavern, and
realized the infeasibility of the initial

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:31.920
plan.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:36.310
In fact, it would never have been possible
to cram such a large number of human beings

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:37.780
into such a small space.

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:43.050
In addition, getting beds through inside narrow
corridors would have been a difficult task,

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:47.380
on par with handling telecommunications in
the absence of radio contact.

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:52.160
Last but not least, some of the doors that
were supposed to seal the tunnels seemed to

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:54.250
encounter problems in closing.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:59.610
In 2006, the housing capacity of the Sonnenberg
Bunker was downsized, and it’s estimated

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:04.150
that it can currently accommodate two thousand
people in case of need.

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:06.209
There is another problem, however.

00:10:06.209 --> 00:10:11.380
According to guide Zora Schelbert, this bunker
can certainly come in handy in case of natural

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:16.910
disasters such as landslides or earthquakes,
but not in case of a nuclear explosion.

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:21.339
After all, the shelter is meant to serve as
a base of support for only a few weeks.

00:10:21.339 --> 00:10:25.120
A time frame too short for the radiation,
outside, to thin out.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:29.860
At present, the main function performed by
the Tunnel is as a tourist attraction, and

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:35.990
in recent decades hundreds of unused bunkers,
some dating back to the 1940s, have been converted

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:42.790
into museums, architectural works, warehouses,
mushroom farms, hotels and data centers, and

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:44.209
even migrant shelters.

00:10:44.209 --> 00:10:49.779
An inauspicious end, you might say, but certainly
dictated by the changing geopolitical environment.

00:10:49.779 --> 00:10:56.940
Although between 1982 and 1983 the Swiss government
had invested $28 per capita in the defense

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:02.060
sector, ten more than the entire Soviet Union,
the threats of the Cold War, and with it the

00:11:02.060 --> 00:11:04.760
fear of atomic attack, soon vanished.

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:09.800
From 1989, the rate of shelter construction
declined but not the debate over the nation's

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:11.220
total defense.

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:16.310
During the 1980s, the Swiss parliament began
to show its first doubts about fortifying

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:21.800
the country, and a pacifist ideology, critical
of the militaristic policies pursued by the

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:26.889
federal government in previous decades, became
prevalent among the intellectuals of the time.

00:11:26.889 --> 00:11:32.950
Swiss journalist Jean-Gabriel Zufferey summed
them up in 1989 with the term "hedgehog syndrome."

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:39.019
Then the 1990s passed, and the attack on the
Twin Towers on September 11, 2001 generated

00:11:39.019 --> 00:11:41.530
tremendous insecurity throughout the West.

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:47.450
When, in 2005, Congressman Pierre Kohler proposed
to abolish the requirement to build bunkers

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:52.671
adjacent to private homes to avoid unnecessarily
increasing the cost of real estate, the Federal

00:11:52.671 --> 00:11:57.910
Council decided that the shelters were still
useful in defending the population from terrorist

00:11:57.910 --> 00:11:58.910
attacks.

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:03.740
The issue was reignited in March 2011, following
the Fukushima nuclear disaster.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:09.000
However, during this time, Swiss authorities
have gradually relaxed the penalties for violating

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:11.310
the Federal Law on Civil Protection.

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:16.670
According to a new regulation that came into
effect in 2012 and was confirmed in 2022,

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:20.940
only buildings with 38 or more rooms will
have to have a fallout shelter.

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:25.930
If it’s impossible for technical reasons
to build one, an annual replacement fee of

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:32.880
800 Swiss francs, about 895 dollars at today's
exchange rate, will have to be paid to the

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:33.880
state.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:37.959
The same fee is paid by all other property
owners who don’t want to make a home shelter,

00:12:37.959 --> 00:12:40.540
to guarantee them a place in a public bunker.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:45.199
The latter, however, must be located in the
proximity of homes, that is, a distance that

00:12:45.199 --> 00:12:47.120
can be walked in half an hour.

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:52.010
According to current regulations, a bunker
must be built according to the technical instructions

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:57.699
issued by the Federal Department of Defense
in 2017, which indicate materials, measures

00:12:57.699 --> 00:13:02.480
and maintenance activities to be followed
for every facility built within the structures,

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:07.920
from security doors to kitchens, from ventilation
and communication systems to toilets and light

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:08.920
bulbs.

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:13.930
For the past year, Russia's invasion of Ukraine
and the feared use of tactical nuclear weapons

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:16.730
has again alarmed Bern and the cantons.

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:21.410
According to Stephen Herzog, a nuclear weapons
expert working at the Swiss Federal Institute

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:26.820
of Technology in Zurich, Switzerland is currently
the country best prepared for a possible atomic

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:32.490
conflict, not only because of the 370.000
bunkers scattered throughout its territory,

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:37.080
but also because of the excellent level of
nuclear and radiological protection provided

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:38.080
by them.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:42.480
The whole of Switzerland, then, is covered
by more than 7.000 sirens, which are also

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:47.149
activated in case of more localized disasters,
such as fires or floods.

00:13:47.149 --> 00:13:52.600
Finally, the Alert Swiss app, available since
2019, informs the population about current

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:55.990
alerts, and was also functional during the
Covid-19 pandemic.

00:13:55.990 --> 00:14:01.450
In short, it would be fair to say that, somehow,
the strategy of Redoubt envisioned in the

00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:04.220
late 1800s has survived to the present day.

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:09.100
Of course, it has changed guises: it has become
the armor of a small country squeezed between

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:14.130
the Alps, but diplomatically and economically
at the pinnacle of the international order.

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:19.480
Such preparation for any kind of catastrophe
is admirable, as well as unmatched, at present,

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:21.970
for any other state on the face of the Earth.

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:26.430
Sweden and Finland, second and third in class
in the bunker league, have facilities that

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:31.310
can save "only" 70 and 62 percent of their
residents, respectively.

00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:37.680
Some cantons, such as Grisons and Glarus would
be able to shelter 140% of their population.

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:43.690
This is the result of the federal government's
investment of about $12 billion, diluted over

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:44.690
60 years.

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:49.290
All this, of course, doesn’t mean that the
world's most anti-atomic nation is safe from

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:51.029
any apocalyptic scenario.

00:14:51.029 --> 00:14:55.490
Again according to Stephen Herzog, in the
event of a nuclear escalation of the Ukrainian

00:14:55.490 --> 00:15:00.420
conflict, there are so many variables at play
including weather conditions, explosive yield

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:05.300
of weapons, and altitude of detonations that
it’s impossible to determine with certainty

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:09.830
whether the distance to the war zone and the
network of shelters can protect the Swiss

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:11.040
people 100 percent.

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:15.360
But then, is it really possible to survive
nuclear armageddon?

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:19.810
And once the world we are used to living in
is devastated, what will be left of us?

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:23.740
One thing is certain, those who come out to
see the stars again had better have power

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:28.410
armor with them, you can’t know what threats
might be lurking outside the vault.

00:15:28.410 --> 00:15:32.279
Well and we're done for today, I thank you
all for your attention and we’ll see you

00:15:32.279 --> 00:15:33.430
in the next video.

00:15:33.430 --> 00:15:33.770
Ciao!

